          "create_time": 1794583745.804,
          "update_time": 1794583752.212,
          "content": {
            "content_type": "text",
            "parts": [
              "Yes. **The contour determines where we integrate; the observation point determines how those boundary values are weighted.** They are related through the kernel, while remaining separate choices.\n\nWe should distinguish three objects:\n\n- **Integration contour \\(\\Gamma=\\partial D\\):** the boundary surrounding the analysis domain.\n- **Observation point \\(p=X+ib\\):** a point inside that domain where we evaluate the field.\n- **Zero-enclosure boundary \\(y=a\\):** the current upper limit on where zeros could occur.\n\nTheir relationship appears explicitly in\n\\[\nJ_{\\lambda,D}(p)\n=\\int_{\\partial D}\\log|H_\\lambda(\\zeta)|\\,d\\omega_D^p(\\zeta)\n-\\log|H_\\lambda(p)|.\n\\]\nThe boundary weights \\(\\omega_D^p\\) depend on **both the domain and the observation point**. Moving either changes the comparison.\n\nvisualize{\"path\":\"/Users/ebbaker/.codex/visualizations/2026/11/13/01a1d570-d742-77e2-94e1-780baac55672/contour-and-observation.html\"}\n\nIn our proposal, the observation sits above the known zero enclosure, and the contour extends below the layer we want to exclude. **There is no general requirement that the observation be at the contour’s midpoint**; the earlier \\(B=8,\\ b=4\\) choice was convenient.\n\nThe small Cauchy circles used for derivative estimates are separate auxiliary contours, centered at each evaluation point.\n\nI saved this distinction in [Note04](/Users/ebbaker/Documents/shifted-zeta-positivity/papers/investigations-5/09_green_potentials_newman_flow/02_height_time_boundary_bootstrap/notes/04_GNP_BB_CONTOUR_AND_OBSERVATION_CLARIFICATION_20261113.md) and linked it into the continuation guidance."
            ]
          },
